Mass, volume, density, and flow as connected measurements (@met-mass-flow: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @metrology, #source "../sources/Weighing-Calibration.lingua#r_5VF8R8DG8XMTSHWXG8Y32AJYCQ", #source "../sources/Volume-Calibration.lingua#r_1Y7BMP63V5GF7963V2HRNT3917", #source "../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E", #metrology "../practice/Reference-life-cycle.lingua#r_1ZS7B98GYPWX6S7BFZ5S6CY6YR", #fluid-dynamics "../../../physics/fluid-dynamics/measurement/Flow-rate-calibration-density-and-the-installed-meter.lingua#r_2AVBCMG4JY9YW3BW5GWA1J40Y1") { r_7S09P54V6ADP28CA0FTZ6SMHQZ
**Read:** [Calibration of non-automatic weighing instruments — EURAMET cg-18](../sources/Weighing-Calibration.lingua#r_5VF8R8DG8XMTSHWXG8Y32AJYCQ), the complete guide; [Gravimetric volume calibration — EURAMET cg-19](../sources/Volume-Calibration.lingua#r_1Y7BMP63V5GF7963V2HRNT3917), the complete guide; [Simple Guide for Evaluating and Expressing Uncertainty — Possolo](../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E), E1 Weighing and a related model example.

Weighing is a comparison influenced by calibration, buoyancy, load position, zero, sensitivity, repeatability, environment, and handling. Distinguish mass from force and conventional mass from other specified mass quantities. A balance indication must be interpreted with the particular reference and use model.

In an idealized buoyancy model, a reference-calibrated mass indication I can relate to object mass as m = I(1 − ρ_air/ρ_ref)/(1 − ρ_air/ρ_object), before other corrections. Define what I represents and the assumptions behind this expression. Air and material density and their uncertainties can matter; a low-density object and a dense reference do not experience the same buoyant effect.

Volume inferred gravimetrically uses a corrected mass and a density under specified conditions, with handling, evaporation, thermal expansion, and reference-volume conventions as appropriate. “One gram equals one millilitre” is an approximation for some water conditions, not a universal physical identity. Flow adds elapsed time, temporal behavior, and collection or delivery definitions.

**Work:** read the full weighing and volume guides. Reconstruct one uncertainty example from each using its actual model. Create a simplified teaching comparison to show the effect of density and air buoyancy, then add the conditions the simplified model omitted. For a flow study, distinguish average collected volume per interval from instantaneous flow and preserve the timing uncertainty and possible correlation.

Connect the result to a delivery or process balance. A perfectly characterized small weighed portion does not establish a heterogeneous shipment’s average composition, and a collection procedure can miss retained or evaporated material. Report the boundary and measurement definition so the upstream and receiving accounts remain comparable.

**Continue:** [Produce, preserve, transfer, and replace the references](../practice/Reference-life-cycle.lingua#r_1ZS7B98GYPWX6S7BFZ5S6CY6YR).

